  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/2/27/Amazing_Spider-Man_Vol_1_13.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "Spider-Man appears to rob a store, then escape from the pursuing police.&nbsp; Has he turned to crime?&nbsp; People debate the issue while reading the news reports, except that Flash Thompson doesn't appear convinced, and even Peter Parker himself begins to wonder if he's developed a split-personality.&nbsp; He's even afraid to go to sleep, wondering if these things happen after he drops off at night.&nbsp; He needs to find out, so he visits a psychiatrist, but the thought of becoming too relaxed and revealing his identity makes him change his mind at the last minute.\n\nSoon, at the Bugle, Peter goes to see Jameson and ask for a loan to help pay the mortgage -- because Aunt May's bank savings account is dangerously low -- but Jameson refuses, telling him he only pays for photos.&nbsp; Peter decides to go out as Spider-Man and see if he can take some, but he soon finds that the public is outraged by his appearance.&nbsp; It's worse than he imagined:&nbsp; citizens seem to <i>hate</i> him, making it impossible for him to show his face in public (and thus unable to get the money he needs).\n\nThe next day at school, Peter worries about money and whether he's committing crimes in his sleep when Liz Allan comes up to show him her new hairdo.&nbsp; He can't believe that she seems to be paying attention to him now, apparently only because he's dating Betty, but Flash sees Liz as well (while defending Spider-Man to his friends) and puts his foot in his mouth, causing an icy response from Liz.\n\nAt Jameson's office, an oddly-costumed figure arrives and identifies himself as \"Mysterio,\" promising to get rid of Spider-Man single-handedly.&nbsp; Before disappearing in a cloud of smoke, he demands that the Bugle print a notice for Spider-Man to meet him at the top of the Brooklyn Bridge.&nbsp; Sure enough, when Spider-Man arrives, Mysterio is there to greet him and immediately begins to demonstrate how his power is unique.&nbsp; He dissolves Spider-Man's webbing in mid-air and then disappears in a cloud of mist, punching Spider-Man from inside it, who finds his spider sense inexplicably jammed.&nbsp; Finally, Spider-Man sees he's getting nowhere and takes off, leaving Mysterio triumphant.\n\nSoon, a motorcade honors Mysterio as the man who defeated Spider-Man, but Flash Thompson watches, unconvinced, still telling everyone that Spider-Man is innocent.&nbsp; Even Peter seems surprised at Flash's refusal to believe the worst about his hero.\n\nBefore long, Jameson introduces Mysterio to the members of the Bugle staff.&nbsp; He's excited that Mysterio has agreed to give the Bugle an exclusive of him defeating and then unmasking Spider-Man after their next encounter, but when Mysterio shakes hands with Peter Parker (whom Jameson calls \"the best photographer I've got\"), Peter slips a spider-tracer into his cloak.\n\nIt isn't long before Spider-Man tracks him down near a \"TV movie studio building.\"&nbsp; But Mysterio instantly summons the mist that dulls his spider sense, and seeing that Spider-Man is no threat to him, admits to impersonating him.&nbsp; He reveals that he was a movie stunt man who \"became a special effects man for TV movies.\"&nbsp; He could design and mimic almost anything, so he decided to imitate Spider-Man and commit crimes as him, using a separate identity as Mysterio for himself.&nbsp; He even explains to Spider-Man how his suit works, revealing its host of concealed devices inside that he uses to simulate magic.\n\nLittle does he know that Spider-Man has been taping the entire monologue!&nbsp; But Mysterio doesn't care because he's going to <i>destroy</i> Spider-Man.&nbsp; But this time, Spider-Man stays outside the cloud and repeatedly punches into it until he finally connects, and the two spill into a nearby movie set and engage!&nbsp; Mysterio is indeed a skilled stunt man and is able to roll with many of the punches he takes, but Spider-Man finally destroys the jamming device that dulls his spider sense, and thus is able to connect with Mysterio even through the cloud he tries to hide in.\n\nAnd this time, Peter has remembered to set up his automatic camera to take photos, so he captured all the action on film.\n\nSoon, the defeated Mysterio sits at police headquarters, having been dropped there by Spider-Man, complete with his full confession on tape.&nbsp; Jameson arrives to learn that Spider-Man is innocent after all and is dejected once again.&nbsp; He'll be a \"laughing stock,\" but his attitude changes when he returns to the Bugle to find the action photos Peter Parker has dropped off -- he's saved!\n\nHe decides to be \"generous and pay him [Peter] almost <i>half</i> of what these pictures are worth!\"&nbsp; But Spider-Man arrives to tell him off and express his amazement at how he manages to be 100% wrong about him <i>every single time -</i>- and naturally to web him to the ceiling to boot!\n\nBack at school, Flash Thompson is triumphant, having been proved right about Spider-Man, even while Peter Parker pretends not to trust the man \"any further than I can throw him!\"\n\n<i>Oh</i>, if only Flash \"knew the real identity of his favorite hero!\"&nbsp; His head would <i>surely</i> explode!",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "[ASSET: left | TurningToCrime | width=12%] [ASSET: right | SplitPersonality | width=12%]&nbsp; <b>FIXME</b>\n\nThe visit to the psychiatrist was hyped on the cover, but as the whole sequence lasted a mere 4-5 panels in the actual story (spanning pages 4 and 5), it amounted to almost nothing, giving the impression that it was just a passing, \"wouldn't-it-be-interesting\" moment of discussion moment exchanged between Stan and Steve, but ultimately with no meaningful payoff.\n\n[ASSET: left | MomentWithBetty | width=12%] What <i>did</i> actually have more impact was the terse way that Peter spoke to Betty on page 5.&nbsp; Seeing Peter so caught up in his feelings and fear of actually <i>being</i> a criminal without even knowing it, resulting in him speaking in such a tone to Betty (shocking her, as you can see in the panel), <i>was</i> a far moment potent moment.&nbsp; Reader double-take at his careless words is unavoidable, yet this <i>too</i> is quickly brushed aside, and no mention of its consequences is ever given.\n\nIt's as if the co-creators had a checklist and were marking things off as they went.\n[HANG] * Spider-Man apparently turns bad\n[HANG] * The public is confused and feels betrayed\n[HANG] * But not Flash Thompson -- he's still a staunch supporter of his masked hero\n[HANG] * Aunt May is still poor and struggles to pay the mortgage\n[HANG] * Peter is so worried, he briefly considers psychological treatment\n[HANG] * The worry gets to him, causing him to speak carelessly to Betty\n[HANG] * Jameson refuses to lend him money\n[HANG] * The public is after Spider-Man, making it impossible to snap photos to make money\n[HANG] * Liz has a new hairdo and wants Peter to notice -- Flash inserts foot into mouth\n[HANG] * A creep named Mysterio offers to \"end the menace of Spider-Man\"\n[HANG] * The <i>Daily Bugle</i> announcement prompts the hero to meet him at the Brooklyn Bridge.\n\nThe sense of being carried relentlessly forward like a barreling freight train -- stopping for <i>nothing</i> -- is real.\n[BR] And yet, it does allow the co-creators to quickly consume nearly half the book.\n\nSpider-Man <i>does</i> finally arrive to confront Mysterio at the top of the Brooklyn Bridge, who immediately betrays his true motive when he says (in <i>literally </i>the first words out of his mouth), \"I am <i>Mysterio</i>!&nbsp; I am the one who will single-handedly <i>destroy</i> Spider-Man!\"&nbsp; (Never mind that he instructed the <i>Bugle</i> to print an announcement promising that Spider-Man would \"learn the <i>truth</i> about himself\")&nbsp; Not at <i>all</i> subtle, is he?\n\n\n\n[ASSET: left | IcyResponse | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n(And I really like the word balloon with little icicles hanging off the bottom - very well done!)\n\n\n[ASSET: right | Mysterio | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: center | MeetingMysterio | width=large] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: center | Monologue | width=large] <b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: right | ManyFacesOfJonah | width=12%] <b>FIXME</b>\n(his reaction on the last panel of page 21 is predictably hilarious - \"I'm saved!\" - heh).\n\n\nA solid enough introduction to one of Spider-Man's rogues gallery.&nbsp; A glory-seeking movie stunt man and special-effects guy turned to crime.&nbsp; It was a pretty insidious plan he came up with to first frame Spider-Man and then offer to nab him (for compensation of course).&nbsp; But naturally the monologue at the end did him in (as is always the case).\n\nI'll say it now for the record -- I've never considered Mysterio all that interesting.&nbsp; I first encountered him in the pages of this mag as the book approached issue 200, and he didn't impress me then and still doesn't.&nbsp; But he is considered one of Spider-Man's main enemies, and I know he pops up here and there going forward, and so I will try to treat him with the respect that a mainstay character demands.\n\nAs is often the case, the book got a significant boost from its supporting cast.&nbsp; Aunt May's money struggles, the hint that the Liz Allan/Betty Brant situation might get complicated, Jameson's continued efforts to discredit Spider-Man, and Flash Thompson showing strong support for his hero even while all others had lost faith.&nbsp; These little things added up to an extra boost in the bottom line -- the book would otherwise have been right around mid-grade..&nbsp; But this mag continues to demonstrate how these side things are often just as important as the main plot-of-the-month, and it's a great aspect that seems missing in some of the other Marvel mags and makes the <i>Amazing Spider-Man </i>a solid read for the most part, issue after issue.\n\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\n<b>FIXME</b>",
  "side_note": [
    "The top of page 10 has the phrase \"proportionate strength and agility of the invertebrate for whom he has named himself...\"&nbsp; Spider-Man used the more common version of this phrase in issue #1 when he took on the Fantastic Four, but I don't recall having read this since (I might be wrong), so perhaps Stan finally decided, over a year later, that it was a good phrase to periodically throw into the text -- just wanted to point that out."
  ],
  "letters_page": "I laughed out loud when I read Marcelaine Wininger's letter that pleaded \"Don't let Aunt May die, please!&nbsp; It would ruin everything!\"&nbsp; (Nice to see such passion over a supporting cast member!)\n\nSteve Gillespie's letter also invoked a laugh when he stated:&nbsp; \"The one thing wrong with your comic is Ditko's art.&nbsp; He draws Spider-Man and the super villains fine, but the way he draws people makes them look like they have some rare disease!\" -- to which the editor replies:&nbsp; <i>They have!&nbsp; It's called \"Ditko-itis.</i>\n\nHe's not wrong:&nbsp; Ditko <i>does</i> draw people kind of funny, and I've never been a big fan, but I've already gone on record to say that, for this magazine, and for these characters in particular, he really <i>has</i> nailed it.&nbsp; The quirky art here (and with Doctor Strange) just \"works\" -- far more than it does anywhere else.&nbsp; Even David Catena agrees, in his letter from this issue, which includes \"I realize now that you and only you can draw Spider-Man the way he should be drawn.\"&nbsp; (And anyone who has seen the Kirby or Ayers renditions of him <i>must</i> be forced to agree -- they really <i>are</i> sub-par.)",
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}